
Here is a step-by-step breakdown of how a public VPC with an EC2 instance is configured in the video Day 27: Configuring Public VPC with EC2 for Internet Access | 100 Days of AWS | KodeKloud Engineer:

1. Create a Custom VPC
Action: Navigate to the AWS VPC Console and select Create VPC (VPC only) [00:00:33].

Details: Name the VPC datacenter-pub-vpc and set the IPv4 CIDR block to 10.0.0.0/16.

2. Create and Configure Subnet
Action: Create a subnet inside datacenter-pub-vpc named datacenter-pub-subnet with the CIDR block 10.0.0.0/24 [00:01:42].

Auto-assign Public IP: Edit the subnet settings and enable Auto-assign public IPv4 address so instances created in this subnet automatically receive a public IP [00:02:03].

3. Setup Internet Gateway & Routing
Subnet Association: Associate the route table with the newly created subnet [00:03:07].

Internet Gateway: Create an Internet Gateway named datacenter public internet gateway [00:04:12] and attach it to datacenter-pub-vpc [00:04:26].

Route Entry: Edit the route table rules to add a route for 0.0.0.0/0 targeting the attached Internet Gateway, enabling public internet routing [00:04:59].

4. Launch EC2 Instance
Action: Navigate to the EC2 Dashboard and launch a new instance named datacenter-pub-ec2 using Amazon Linux and instance type t2.micro [00:05:37].

Network Settings: Select datacenter-pub-vpc and datacenter-pub-subnet.

Security Group: Create a security group allowing inbound SSH (Port 22) and HTTP traffic [00:06:59].

5. Verify Internet Access
Connection: Connect to the instance using EC2 Instance Connect [00:07:53].

Test: Execute a ping command to confirm that the instance has outbound internet access [00:08:36].


